==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm}} {{term|targe|lang=enm}}, from {{etyl|ang}} {{term|targa|lang=ang}} {{m}} and {{term|targe|lang=ang}} {{f}}, both from {{etyl|non}} {{term|targa|lang=non}} "round shield" from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{recons|targōn||edge|lang=gem-pro}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{recons|dArg'h-||fenced lot|lang=ine-pro}}. Akin to {{etyl|goh|-}} {{term|zarga|lang=goh}} "side wall, rim" ({{de}} {{term|Zarge|lang=de}} "border, frame"). Reinforced in Middle English by Old French {{term||targe|lang=fro}} (French {{term||targe|lang=fr}}, Spanish ''[[tarja]]''), from the same Germanic source.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/tɑːd͡ʒ/}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|/tɑɹd͡ʒ/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{archaic}} A small [[shield]].
#* '''1819''', The Jester wore his usual fantastic habit, but late accidents had led him to adopt a good cutting falchion, instead of his wooden sword, with a '''targe''' to match it — Walter Scott, ''Ivanhoe''

